5:[["$","$L10","5.1",{"topic":{"id":"5.1","title":"Introduction to Cyber Threat Intelligence","minutes":11,"summary":"Cyber threat intelligence is decision support about adversaries, capabilities, and risk—not an indiscriminately collected list of bad IP addresses.","sections":[{"title":"From data to actionable intelligence","body":["Threat intelligence is analyzed information that helps a decision-maker understand and act on cyber risk. Raw logs, indicators, incident reports, and actor profiles become intelligence when their relevance, reliability, and implications are evaluated. A list of domains without context may support blocking, but it does not explain the actor, campaign, or business risk.","Strategic intelligence supports leadership and policy; operational intelligence supports campaigns and defensive priorities; tactical intelligence describes techniques and behaviors; technical intelligence includes indicators and detection details. These categories overlap. The product should match its audience rather than giving every reader the same technical feed."]},{"title":"Know the consumer and the decision","body":["A security operations analyst may need a detection rule with validation guidance. An incident responder may need likely persistence mechanisms. A finance leader may need exposure and mitigations. Begin with the decision, required confidence, timeframe, and acceptable action. Intelligence that cannot influence a decision is often just additional noise.","Describe the threat actor, capability, infrastructure, motivation, victimology, and observed behavior separately. A shared tool does not prove shared membership. Use confidence labels and source references. The MITRE ATT&CK knowledge base can organize observed techniques, but a technique label is not attribution to a named group."]},{"title":"Reliability, sharing, and legal handling","body":["Distinguish source reliability from information credibility. A normally reliable source can repeat an unverified claim. Record whether an observation is confirmed, reported, inferred, or unknown. Sharing frameworks such as STIX, TAXII, and MISP can help structure exchange, but they do not remove confidentiality, privacy, or licensing obligations.","Threat intelligence can contain personal data, victim information, or sensitive infrastructure details. Use access controls and sharing agreements. Avoid publishing indicators that would expose victims or ongoing operations. A useful intelligence product states its date, scope, confidence, and recommended validation rather than presenting a feed as permanent truth."]}],"practice":{"title":"Write a priority intelligence requirement","scenario":"A university is concerned about credential phishing during admissions.","steps":["Identify the decision-maker and the decision to support.","Define the relevant accounts, period, and threat behavior.","Choose an intelligence product and confidence requirement.","Specify how the result will be validated and shared safely."],"debrief":"A requirement tied to a real decision produces focused collection and a usable result."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","dpdp"],"sources":["first","mitre","nist-csf"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain from data to actionable intelligence and apply it to an investigation.","Explain know the consumer and the decision and apply it to an investigation.","Explain reliability, sharing, and legal handling and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.1","prompt":"What makes threat information actionable?","options":["A long list of indicators without context.","Relevance to a decision, evaluated sources, and usable guidance.","The most alarming headline.","A claim of certainty with no evidence."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Actionable intelligence connects evaluated information to an audience, decision, and appropriate response.","unit":5}}}]]
